                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_decision:      `<strong>That is the real brief.</strong> The restructuring is stalling because managers won't decide in the new structure — so the program's job is to change a <em>decision behaviour</em>, not deliver leadership content. Now design backwards from that, and pick a metric that proves it moved.`,
                    coach_r1_content:       `<strong>You've just re-sold us last year's course.</strong> A polished leadership-essentials program is almost exactly what we bought and called “lovely, and we have no idea what it did.” I didn't ask for content — I asked for a result. Why would managers behaving the same way unstick a stalled restructuring?`,
                    coach_r1_engagement:    `<strong>Morale isn't the business problem.</strong> Engagement scores might wobble, but the CEO's pain is that the reorg has stalled. Get to <em>why</em> — managers aren't deciding in the new structure — or you'll design a feel-good program that misses the result.`,
                    coach_r1_skills:        `<strong>“They lack leadership skills generally” is the vague-brief trap.</strong> Broadening the curriculum spreads thin across competencies none of which is the actual blocker. Diagnose the specific behaviour the restructuring needs before you design anything.`,
                    coach_r1_happy:         `<strong>A satisfaction score is exactly why I can't tell the CEO what the last program did.</strong> Beating a 4.6/5 happy sheet proves nothing about the business. What number would the CEO accept as evidence the reorg unstuck?`,
                    coach_r1_skip:          `<strong>Skipping the second discovery call to “protect the 90 days” designs blind.</strong> You'll commit design dollars against the brief's words, not the real driver. A short, sharp diagnostic call is what surfaces where decisions actually stall.`,
                    coach_r1_lowconviction: `<strong>You're hedging back toward last year's course.</strong> If you won't firmly reject re-selling content, the design will drift to the comfortable, generic program — the very thing that left us unable to name an outcome.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the real problem — decision paralysis in the new structure — and chose decision-speed as the proof. That is designing backwards from the business result. Now build a format that can actually move it inside my budget and my quarter.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>Blended is the modelled trade-off.</strong> Online modules plus live workshops plus action-learning on the real restructuring gets a genuine behaviour-change program live in 90 days, inside C$5,000 a head. Make sure the reinforcement is enough to embed the new decision behaviour.`,
                    coach_r2_feasible:      `<strong>That fits the budget and the date</strong> — good. Now check it's rich enough to change the decision behaviour, not just deliver content. A feasible but light design hits the deadline and misses the result.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>Add it up — you're over C$5,000 a head.</strong> In-person days, faculty fees and individual touch push you past the ceiling, and we approved C$750,000 and nothing more. Either the format gets leaner or the cohort gets smaller. Which, and why is that the right cut for the outcome you promised?`,
                    coach_r2_cantlaunch:    `<strong>This is a beautiful build that can't launch in 90 days.</strong> It takes longer than the quarter and our faculty pool is booked. Cascadia launches this quarter or walks. What format gets a real behaviour-change program live in 90 days — and what do you give up to hit the date?`,
                    coach_r2_cohort:        `<strong>You trimmed the cohort to protect the budget.</strong> Fewer managers is a legitimate cut — but the CEO asked for ~150, and every manager you drop is one still stuck in the old decision habits. Be ready to defend why the smaller group is the right cut for the outcome.`,
                    coach_r2_offshelf:      `<strong>That's mostly off-the-shelf content.</strong> Heavy reuse hits the date and the budget, but generic material won't change the <em>specific</em> decision behaviour the restructuring needs. Keep enough genuinely bespoke design to move our actual problem.`,
                    coach_r3_l4:            `<strong>Measuring through to results is the discipline we skipped.</strong> Reaction, learning, behaviour AND a restructuring-linked business result — that's a definition of success the CEO can act on. Now make sure the baseline and attribution can defend the headline number.`,
                    coach_r3_l1:            `<strong>Satisfaction and completion is exactly what the last program measured</strong> — and it's why I can't tell the CEO what it did. A happy sheet is not a result. What will you measure at the behaviour and business levels, and what's the baseline you take before day one?`,
                    coach_r3_nobaseline:    `<strong>No baseline means no provable impact.</strong> If you only measure after the program, you can never show what changed — the prior program's fatal omission. Capture decision-speed and behaviour <em>before</em> day one or the result is unprovable.`,
                    coach_r3_overclaim:     `<strong>You've told the CEO a 30% decision-speed lift, all of it yours.</strong> I'll ask how you separate your workshops from the reorg itself, the new incentives and the org-chart change. An over-claim you can't defend destroys trust faster than a modest claim you can. What can you honestly attribute, and how?`,
                    coach_r3_sharp:         `<strong>That is a defensible measurement plan.</strong> Levels through results, a pre-program baseline, and a headline metric isolated from the reorg via a control group — that's a number the CEO can believe and you can stand behind. This is what the last program never had.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Holding C$750,000 is the right call — if you make the case.</strong> Northline's whole premise is that it's worth more because it proves outcomes. Don't just hold the number; show the CEO the baselined result this design produces that the boutique's cheaper scope can't.`,
                    coach_r4_split:         `<strong>Splitting to C$640,000 by trimming measurement is the worst cut.</strong> Measurement is the very thing that proves the outcome and justifies the premium. You've shaved the price by removing your own evidence — defend the full design or you've conceded the boutique's logic.`,
                    coach_r4_match:         `<strong>You dropped to C$540,000 to beat the boutique.</strong> You've just thrown away Northline's margin and conceded you're interchangeable with a cheaper vendor. Our whole premise is that we're worth more because we prove outcomes. Make the impact case — or explain why you're now a discount provider.`,
                    coach_r4_outcome:       `<strong>That's the pitch that wins on outcomes.</strong> Design, metric and price as one case — a baselined decision-speed result the CEO sees this quarter, at C$750,000, that the boutique's scope can't prove. Hold the line and the renewal follows the result.`,
                    coach_r4_reputation:    `<strong>“Best brand and world-class faculty” is exactly the claim I can't take to the CEO.</strong> Reputation is what we sold last time, and it left us unable to name an outcome. Lead with the result your design proves, not the logo.`,
                    coach_r4_hide:          `<strong>Naming no risk reads as either naïve or hiding something.</strong> The CEO has been burned by training before. The credible move is to name your single biggest delivery risk and the mitigation — that's what earns the premium, not a flawless-sounding pitch.`,

