                    insight_architect:      `Outcomes Architect: you designed backwards from the business result — a decision-behaviour brief, a feasible on-budget blended format, a baselined Levels 1–4 plan, and a price held on outcomes. Cascadia can finally name what the program will do, which is what defends the renewal.`,
                    insight_undeliverable:  `Undeliverable design: the program either blew the C$5,000-per-head budget or couldn't launch in 90 days. A brilliant program Cascadia can't afford or can't start this quarter is a program they won't buy — feasibility is a design constraint, not an afterthought.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully connect the four decisions. The reframed need, the format, the measurement and the pitch still tell slightly different stories — enough that the CEO can't quite see one outcome from need to proof.`,
                    insight_margin_negative: `Your engagement margin is negative: the design cost more per head than the price supports, or you discounted below your cost. Northline runs a 22% margin — a program that loses money sets a precedent worse than losing the deal.`,
                    insight_margin_strong:  `Strong margin: you delivered a behaviour-change program inside the C$5,000-per-head ceiling and held the C$750,000 price — protecting Northline's premium on outcomes rather than buying the deal.`,
                    insight_overclaim:      `You over-claimed the headline impact. A 30% decision-speed lift attributed entirely to your workshops invites the question you can't answer — how you separate the program from the reorg. A modest, defensible claim builds more trust than an unprovable big one.`,
                    insight_unprovable:     `Your impact case stayed weak: too shallow on the Kirkpatrick levels, or no pre-program baseline. Without behaviour and business measurement against a baseline, you've rebuilt the exact program that left Cascadia unable to name an outcome.`,
                    insight_discounted:     `You discounted to win. Matching or nearly matching the boutique surrenders Northline's margin and concedes you're interchangeable with a cheaper vendor — the opposite of the outcomes-based premium the whole engagement was meant to defend.`,
                    insight_trusted:        `High renewal confidence and a strong impact case — Cascadia can name the outcome and trusts the design to deliver it. That is the disclosure-of-impact gap closed, which is exactly what reverses Northline's slipping renewal rate.`,

                    hint_overbudget:        `You're over the C$5,000-per-head ceiling — Cascadia approved no overage. Lean the format (more reuse, blended over bespoke in-person) or trim the cohort, and defend the cut.`,
